# Superpowers subagent-driven-development scratch (progress ledger, task
# briefs, review packages) - workflow state, not project content
.superpowers/

# Crew's per-repo working directory. In a consuming repo this holds
# HANDOFF.md and the work index; in THIS repo it is only ever scratch --
# review diffs and QA output, which is where the 2.2 MB came from.
.work/

# Cisco Meraki skill — config snapshots and bootstrap cache (may contain
# network config and shared secrets; never commit)
.meraki-snapshots/

# Per-repo crew state. Machine-specific by nature: obsidian.vaultPath and
# boardDir are absolute paths that exist on one machine, and pm.authority is a
# trust decision its owner makes, not one a clone should inherit. Treat it like
# .vscode/settings.json. A fresh clone runs /crew:init to generate its own.
.crew/

# Written by the GitHub Copilot CLI when --model is passed; pins the model for
# anyone running `copilot` in this repo. crew owns provider model choice
# (qa.copilot.model / dev.copilot.model), so this file must not shadow it.
.github/copilot/

# graphify code graph. graph.json is deliberately NOT ignored: .gitattributes
# registers a union merge driver for it, and a union driver only has something
# to merge if both sides are tracked. Everything else here is rebuildable —
# cache/ alone is 4.6 MB and churns on every commit via the post-commit hook.
# Allowlist, not a denylist: a build drops cache/, .graphify_analysis.json,
# .graphify_labels.json(.sig), .graphify_root, GRAPH_REPORT.md, manifest.json
# and a 2.6 MB graph.html, and the set grows with the tool. Enumerating them
# means the next new artifact lands untracked-but-visible and gets committed by
# accident. Ignore the directory and re-admit the one file that must be tracked.
graphify-out/*
!graphify-out/graph.json
